On Tue, 2006-10-17 at 13:54 +0200, Frits de Klark wrote:
> Hello everyone!
> 
> Does anyone have or knows of a schematic representation of Xenomai's
> architecture? By this I mean Xenomai's relationship to Adeos, the
> Linux kernel, etc. all in one drawing. I want to use it to explain a
> total outsider how Xenomai works, but right now I'm not even sure if I
> understand it fully, because I was starting out to draw it myself, but
> I get confused about the facts that Xenomai is effectively a part of
> the kernel (as modules, that is), but at the same time treating the
> kernel as a low priority task. Seems a bit tricky to illustrate from
> my (narrow) point of view.

The functional description attached should help.

- Red: the pieces imported by Adeos/Xenomai into a vanilla kernel for
the whole thing to work.
- Green: two applications running in user-space, one using the VXWorks
emulation skin, the other bound to the POSIX 1003.1b skin.
- Light yellow: the Linux kernel space.
- Blue: the regular Linux subsystems as available from the vanilla
kernel.

The purpose of this picture is to illustrate the basic interactions
between the main components, made of system calls, that flow through the
regular syscall channel implemented by Linux. Adeos interposes to
dispatch the syscalls to the proper components, either the vanilla
handling, or Xenomai, depending on the calling context.
The fact that both the regular glibc and the Xenomai libraries are both
available from a real-time executable is also illustrated.

The internal layering of the Xenomai RTOS component is given in that
doc:
http://www.xenomai.org/documentation/branches/v2.0.x/pdf/Life-with-Adeos.pdf

-- 
Philippe.


